      <article class="legal-prose mt-14">
        <h2 id="verdict">The verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Internet Protocol Television is a delivery method, not a product. Once you know it is just
          addressed packets crossing your broadband and being reassembled at the other end, most of the
          marketing around it stops being mysterious and starts being checkable. Ask what the concurrent
          connection limit is. Ask what happens at renewal. Test the thing at peak time.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to try ours, it is £{site.price} for a month as a one-off payment, with a 7-day
          money-back window and no card kept on file.
          <a href={waLink('Hi, I read the Internet Protocol Television guide and would like to try the one-month plan.')} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Message us on WhatsApp</a>
          and we reply in under two minutes during UK hours.
        </p>
        <p>
          And if the picture still stalls after all that, it is not the protocol. Internet Protocol has
          been quietly working since 1974. It is the router in your hallway, behind the mirror, that
          nobody has turned off since the last general election.
        </p>
